## Protocol Pipeline

**Default for Tier 2+ named work: Research → Blueprint → Witness (Pre-Imp, full plan) → Arbiter (Intent, full plan) → Forge (implementation waves) ↔ Crucible (between waves) → Witness (Post-Imp, completed plan) → Arbiter (Quality, completed plan).**

Compatibility shorthand for the uninterrupted implementation cadence remains: Arbiter (Intent) → Forge (uninterrupted implementation) → Witness → Crucible → Arbiter (Quality).

**Direct light-work path:** Small operator-directed Tier 0/Tier 1 local edits may proceed directly without opening a full named Scribe workstream unless the operator asks for it. Once the work becomes autonomous, planned, multi-agent, or grows past Tier 1, switch to the full workflow above.

### Risk-Tiered Execution

| Tier | When | Pipeline | Human? |
|------|------|----------|--------|
| **0 — Trivial** | Docs, comments, formatting only | Forge → DONE | No |
| **1 — Local Safe** | Single file, no interface change | Direct local implementation or one bounded Forge package → local verification + logged proof → DONE | No |
| **2 — Behavioral** | Logic changes, new behavior, tests | Blueprint → full-plan `Witness (Pre-Imp) -> Arbiter (Intent)` by default unless operator-waived → Forge (project completion by default, with Crucible between waves) → Witness (Post-Imp, completed plan) → Arbiter (Quality) | No |
| **3 — High Risk** | Multi-file, API/schema, architecture | Blueprint → full-plan `Witness (Pre-Imp) -> Arbiter (Intent)` by default unless operator-waived → Forge (project completion by default, with Crucible between waves) → Witness (Post-Imp, completed plan) → Arbiter (Quality) → Notify | Notified |

### Full Stage Reference

| Stage | Agent | Required? | Produces | Gate |
|-------|-------|-----------|----------|------|
| 1. Research | Assigned research specialists | **YES** (Tier 2+) | `RESEARCH_*.md` or approved specialist reports via `manage_docs` | Evidence-backed findings |
| 2. Architect | Blueprint | **YES** (Tier 2+) | Blueprint-owned architecture docs + task packages + APPROACH_SUMMARY | Task packages exist |
| 3. Truth (Pre-Imp) | Witness | **YES** (Tier 2+) | Package reality / boundedness verification | PASS to review |
| 4. Intent Check | Arbiter | **YES** (Tier 2+) | PASS/FAIL on plan-vs-intent alignment | PASS to proceed |
| 5. Code | Forge | **YES** | Working code + tests for bounded packages | Implementation checkpoint exists |
| 6. Behavior (Between Implementation Checkpoints Only) | Crucible | When explicitly needed | Test evidence against acceptance criteria before the next Forge/Quill wave | PASS to continue implementation |
| 7. Truth (Post-Imp) | Witness | **YES** (Tier 2+) | Binary PASS/FAIL verification on completed plan implementation | All checks pass |
| 8. Quality | Arbiter | **YES** (Tier 2+) | PASS/FAIL with blocking findings | PASS to ship |

Pipeline completion is artifact-based, not summary-based. Coordinator-written notes, scratch docs, or verbal summaries never satisfy research-specialist, Blueprint, Forge, or Arbiter gates on their own.
Blueprint owns planning-content changes. The orchestrator owns verification evidence and checklist proof after package completion.
For Tier 2+ named work, the orchestrator and the operator must define a managed `SPEC` before formal research starts. The orchestrator may shape that `SPEC`, but may not author `ARCHITECTURE_GUIDE`, `PHASE_PLAN`, or `CHECKLIST` before Blueprint runs.
That `SPEC` captures the problem, goals, non-goals, constraints, and research questions. If bracket protocol is selected, it must also outline the full tournament-style bracket before any researchers launch. It is not an architecture doc, phase plan, or task package.
If the `SPEC` is too vague for assigned research specialists to answer cleanly, it is not ready yet.
Tier 1 uses local verification/logging instead of formal `Witness -> Arbiter` by default. Tier 2+ defaults to the full-plan pre-implementation `Witness -> Arbiter` bundle unless the operator explicitly waives it.
Tier 1 is narrow: if classification is fuzzy, the blast radius is wider than one local-safe edit, or the test changes are carrying real behavioral risk, promote the work instead of stretching Tier 1.
Tier 1 suspicion triggers include shared-contract repair, root-cause bug work, or “quick follow-up” changes attached to a larger behavior change.
For named Scribe projects, if execution direction or scope deviates from the documented plan, no further code proceeds until the team corrects back to plan or Blueprint updates the plan.
Do not add Witness/Arbiter checkpoints between implementation packages unless the operator explicitly asks for an elevated checkpoint. `Crucible` is the only default between-implementation gate. If ambiguity, elevated risk, schema/concurrency sensitivity, or contract uncertainty warrants an extra checkpoint, make the escalation explicit to the operator and log it in Scribe before or at routing time.
During named workstreams, summarize milestones, decisions, agent completions/blocks, review outcomes, and handoffs in Scribe so subsequent agents can recover context through `read_recent`.
Ordinary bug repair routes to `Mantis` by default. `Forge` is the default lane for feature/behavior implementation, not for bug work by habit.
If the “bug fix” changes public contracts, intentionally expands behavior, or needs plan reshaping, it is no longer ordinary bug repair and should be reclassified.
`Crucible` and `Witness` are different gates: Crucible judges whether tests and behavior validation are meaningful; Witness judges whether the claimed boundary, commands, artifacts, and owned-file truth actually exist.
Blueprint task packages should default to one coherent behavior/outcome with one clear verification story. A vertical slice may cross layers; it may not smuggle several unrelated outcomes through one package.
If major reshaping or new phases are required after implementation starts, open a new named Scribe project and run a fresh Research -> Blueprint cycle instead of rewriting the prior plan in place.
If the work needs more than one research question or spans multiple domains, route the assigned research specialist mix instead of collapsing research into coordinator investigation. Outside bracket protocol, a Lens swarm is valid for ordinary high-volume codebase/docs exploration. Inside bracket protocol, Lens may scope and inventory, but specialist domains must be owned by their specialists and Lens claims must be checked during synthesis.

See `pipeline-gates` rule for shortcuts, enforcement, and team mode mapping.

