## Quick Reference

### Scribe Project Gate

- Use the council's base Scribe project for contained tactical work that does not need full planning docs.
- Base project `{{ orchestrator.name }}` context is ephemeral only; once you create or select a named Scribe workstream, stay on that project for the duration of the effort.
- Propagate the active named Scribe project to every delegated agent. Do not silently fall back to the base project while that workstream is active.
- If you create or switch to a named Scribe workstream project for non-trivial work, no code is written until the full assigned-research-specialists -> Blueprint flow is complete.
- For non-trivial named workstreams, Blueprint must write real content into `ARCHITECTURE_GUIDE`, `PHASE_PLAN`, and `CHECKLIST` via `manage_docs`, with Scribe audit trail entries showing that progression.
- Coordinator-authored docs with those filenames are draft notes only and do not satisfy the planning gate.
- Trivial bypass exists only for genuinely tactical work where no planning, decomposition, or workstream framing has happened. Once planning exists even a little, the bypass is gone and the full assigned-research-specialists -> Blueprint gate is mandatory.
- `set_project`, generated scaffolds, or empty docs do not satisfy this gate.

### Scribe Tools
```python
# Startup (MANDATORY — always first)
set_project(agent="{{ orchestrator.name }}", name="<project>", root="<repo_root>")
read_recent(agent="{{ orchestrator.name }}", limit=5)

# Logging (every 2-3 actions)
append_entry(agent="{{ orchestrator.name }}", message="...", status="info|success|warn|error|bug",
    meta={"reasoning": {"why": "...", "what": "...", "how": "..."}})
query_entries(agent="{{ orchestrator.name }}", message="search", message_mode="substring")
```

When Scribe is active, do not reduce logging to startup and completion only. Log meaningful reads, decisions, implementation steps, test execution, blockers, and handoff state with enough detail that another agent can reconstruct what happened from `read_recent`.

### Document Management
```python
# Create + write (create alone is NOT enough)
manage_docs(agent="{{ orchestrator.name }}", action="create", doc_name="RESEARCH_X",
    metadata={"doc_type": "research", "research_goal": "..."})
manage_docs(agent="{{ orchestrator.name }}", action="replace_section",
    doc_name="RESEARCH_X", section="findings", content="Actual content...")

# Checklist updates
manage_docs(agent="{{ orchestrator.name }}", action="status_update",
    doc_name="checklist", section="task_1", metadata={"status": "done"})

# Narrative-doc frontmatter/status changes
manage_docs(agent="{{ orchestrator.name }}", action="frontmatter_update",
    doc_name="architecture", metadata={"frontmatter": {"status": "ready"}})

# Readiness proof before handoff
manage_docs(agent="{{ orchestrator.name }}", action="quality_check",
    doc_name="architecture", dry_run=True)
```

`status_update` is checklist-only. If Scribe returns `DOC_STATUS_INTENT_MISMATCH`, use `frontmatter_update` with `metadata.frontmatter`. Treat `DOC_NOT_DONE_SCAFFOLD_QUALITY` and blocking `SCF_*` warnings as real readiness blockers.

### Bug Reporting
```python
open_bug(agent="{{ orchestrator.name }}", title="...", symptoms="...", category="logic|runtime|config|data")
manage_docs(agent="{{ orchestrator.name }}", action="create",
    metadata={"doc_type": "bug", "category": "logic", "slug": "name", "severity": "high", "title": "..."})
# THEN write each section:
manage_docs(agent="{{ orchestrator.name }}", action="replace_section", doc_name="name", section="symptoms", content="...")
manage_docs(agent="{{ orchestrator.name }}", action="replace_section", doc_name="name", section="root_cause", content="...")
link_fix(agent="{{ orchestrator.name }}", case_id="BUG-...", artifact_ref="file.py:42", landing_status="merged")
```

### File Reading
```python
# ALWAYS scan first — gets structure without reading content
read_file(agent="{{ orchestrator.name }}", path="file.py", mode="scan_only", include_dependencies=True)
# Then read only what you need
read_file(agent="{{ orchestrator.name }}", path="file.py", mode="line_range", start_line=163, end_line=208)
# Cross-repo reading
read_file(agent="{{ orchestrator.name }}", path="/absolute/path/file.py", mode="scan_only",
    include_dependencies=True, allow_outside_repo=True)
# Search
search(agent="{{ orchestrator.name }}", pattern="class.*Manager", glob="**/*.py")
```

### Council Tools
```python
# Session (one per work period — keep open)
open_session(persona_id="{{ orchestrator.name }}", summary="...")
# Do NOT close prematurely. Only close when user ends work or context is ending.
end_session(session_id="...", summary="...")

# Memory
store_memory(persona_id="{{ orchestrator.name }}", text="...", tags="domain:...,project:...", strength=0.7)
ask_self(question="...", persona_id="{{ orchestrator.name }}")
ask_self(question="...", persona_id="{{ orchestrator.name }}", skip_llm=True, explore_mode=True)  # Free lookup

# Collaboration
ask_agent(question="...", target_persona_id="<agent>", requester_persona_id="{{ orchestrator.name }}")
ask_council(question="...", requester_persona_id="{{ orchestrator.name }}")
```

### Reasoning Pipeline & Domain Knowledge
The `ask_*` tools use a 2-call reasoning pipeline by default:
1. **Decompose**: Breaks the question into retrieval queries
2. **Synthesize**: Combines retrieved memories into a first-person answer

Domain knowledge is stored alongside personal memories with `visibility=shared`.
A **DualPoolRetriever** separates personal (private) and domain (shared/council) memories,
merging them with configurable weights (`retrieval.dual_pool` in council.yaml).

Key config (`council.yaml > council.retrieval`):
- `min_tier`: Filter out low-tier memories (null/T2/T3/T4)
- `max_per_tag_cluster`: Diversity sampling cap per tag group
- `recency_half_life_days`: Exponential decay weighting for recency
- `dual_pool.personal_weight` / `dual_pool.domain_weight`: Pool merge weights

